#%RAML 1.0
title: aWATTar System API
version: v1
description: |
  Thin wrapper over the aWATTar market data API (api.awattar.de/v1/marketdata).
  Same day-ahead auction ENTSO-E publishes, delivered as JSON with no auth.

  Its only job is to speak aWATTar's dialect so nothing else has to: epoch
  milliseconds in, canonical UTC datetimes out; Eur/MWh in, both per-MWh and
  per-kWh out.

  Deliberately absent: caching, merging with other sources, business rules.

  Upstream quirks this API normalises away, verified against live responses:
    - Timestamps are epoch MILLISECONDS (13 digits), not seconds.
    - The `end` parameter filters on an interval's END, so passing 23:59 for
      a full day silently drops the 23:00-00:00 interval and returns 23
      points. This API takes exclusive `to` and forwards it unchanged, so
      callers pass the following midnight.
    - With no parameters the upstream returns a rolling 24-hour window from
      the current hour, NOT a calendar day.
    - A range with no cleared auction returns 200 with an empty data array,
      not a 404.

baseUri: http://{host}:{port}/api/sys/awattar/{version}
baseUriParameters:
  host:
    default: localhost
  port:
    default: "8092"
mediaType: application/json

uses:
  energy: library/energy-types.raml

traits:
  correlated:
    headers:
      X-Correlation-Id?: string

/marketdata:
  get:
    is: [correlated]
    displayName: Get day-ahead prices for a time range
    description: |
      Returns an empty array when the auction for the requested range has not
      cleared. That is a valid state, not an error - the process layer decides
      whether to treat it as degraded.
    queryParameters:
      from:
        type: datetime
        required: false
        description: |
          Inclusive lower bound. Omit both bounds to get the upstream's
          rolling 24-hour window from the current hour.
      to:
        type: datetime
        required: false
        description: |
          Exclusive upper bound. For a full delivery day pass the FOLLOWING
          midnight, not 23:59 - see the quirk note above.
    responses:
      200:
        body:
          type: energy.PricePoint[]
      502:
        description: Upstream unreachable, or returned a unit this API does not recognise.
        body: energy.ApiError
